          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.087,
            0.253,
            0.124
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Threads of Curiosity"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.063,
            0.133,
            0.293,
            0.289
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The word ‘chromatography’ comes from the Greek words chroma, meaning ‘colour’ and graphein, meaning ‘to write’. It actually means ‘writing with colour’ because it was first used to separate coloured substances like dyes and inks."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.322,
            0.089,
            0.644,
            0.109
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5.3.3 Paper Chromatography"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.32,
            0.111,
            0.89,
            0.196
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Have you ever observed what happens when a drop of water falls on something written with a sketch pen on a paper? You may have noticed that the colour spreads. If the sketch pen is black, different colours might spread out. If you have not noticed this before, try it now!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.322,
            0.2,
            0.635,
            0.218
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 5.5: Let us investigate"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.332,
            0.221,
            0.889,
            0.29
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Take a 3 cm wide strip of chromatographic paper and draw a straight, horizontal line, 2 cm from the bottom of the paper with a pencil (Fig. 5.15a). Alternatively, you can use a strip of filter paper."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.333,
            0.292,
            0.888,
            0.327
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Mark a spot with a black sketch pen at the centre of the line (Fig. 5.15b)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.024,
            0.344,
            0.528,
            0.548
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.024,
                0.344,
                0.528,
                0.548
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_10_image_8.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.214,
                0.558,
                0.453,
                0.574
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 5.15: Paper chromatography"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.536,
            0.33,
            0.888,
            0.382
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Take enough water to make a thin layer at the bottom of a gas jar, a measuring cylinder, or a beaker."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.536,
            0.384,
            0.89,
            0.486
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Place the paper strip with the ink spot vertically into the container, so that its lower end dips into the water. The water level should be below the spot, as shown in Fig. 5.15c."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.537,
            0.489,
            0.889,
            0.539
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Observe the paper as the water rises through the paper. What do you notice?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.535,
            0.543,
            0.888,
            0.594
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. As the water rises, the ink starts to separate into different colour spots. What can you infer from this?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.598,
            0.89,
            0.7
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This method of separating the components of a mixture is known as "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "paper chromatography",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". It uses differences in the interactions of the components with the solvent and the paper to separate them. The liquid carries the substances up the paper, separating them based on how fast they move. You can also try this with green food colour instead of black ink and use a 2 % m/v salt solution as the solvent."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.702,
            0.89,
            0.772
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Similarly, you can separate different pigments present in the green extract of spinach leaves or coloured pigments from the flower petals. Will water work as a solvent in every case? In some cases, you may need to use a different solvent, such as alcohol or a mixture of solvents."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.772,
            0.19,
            0.804
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 16,
              "bbox": [
                0.132,
                0.772,
                0.19,
                0.804
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_10_image_16.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.196,
            0.78,
            0.381,
            0.797
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pause and Ponder"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.123,
            0.808,
            0.862,
            0.825
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. State whether the following statements are True or False. Also, correct the False statements."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.826,
            0.761,
            0.842
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) Salt can be separated from a salt solution by evaporation or distillation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.842,
            0.863,
            0.872
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) Distillation can be used for separation of two liquids even when these have the same boiling point."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.872,
            0.863,
            0.903
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iii) In paper chromatography, the solvent level should be above the sample spot at the beginning of the experiment."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.142,
            0.903,
            0.634,
            0.92
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iv) Evaporation and crystallization are the same processes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.079,
            0.943,
            0.108,
